AB  - Large Language Models as Web scrapers cause a massive threat to the news and the media houses by even permitting the unauthorized extraction, reinterpretation, and even use of proprietary materials. Unlike traditional bots, these are sophisticated scrapers which use semantic reasoning, browser computerization, and simulation of human navigation behaviour, in this way beating conventional anti-bot defenses and augmenting the reputational risk because of hallucinated or falsely attributed content. This is a proxy based multi-layered proxy called Armos. middleware model which integrates active HTTP header. fingerprinting, behavioral monitor-TLS/JA3 anomaly detection. ing, honeypot-based deception, and machine learning-based deception. driven classification. Working as a mediating inspection. layer, Armos will allow detecting in real time and counter-adaptively. termeasures on malicious traffic by blocking, throttling, or premeditated fraudulent reaction. We perform an experimental assessment of its efficacy: alone, the use of static fingerprinting produced Accuracy on known bot and LLM client signatures is 97.8 percent and the behavioral and honeypot systems detected 91 percent of the. activities of adaptive LLM scrapers out of their inelastic and low-variance. timing patterns, such as 0.9 sec mean request interviewe. compared to 7.8 sec by humans. The final XGBoost incumbent. generated 96.5 % validation accuracy whose characteristics in Header. Assumptions of entropy, JA3 Mismatch and Behavioral Anomaly Score. greater than 70 percent of predictive value. These findings verify that Armos should provide a stalwart defense and at the same time retain fair authentication of authorized access, and its intrinsic feedback of learning loop will also ensure flexibility to varying scraping techniques and improve the safety of the digital journalism content within the times of generative AI. © 2026 IEEE.
